    {
      "id": "69895f13-da0e-46e8-b38f-f084521c2912",
      "name": "Building Tools with MCP: A Practical Guide",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Building Tools with MCP: A Practical Guide",
      "docTypeId": "229a78fc-a2c4-40c3-81f6-f4b4154e4034",
      "templateId": "1f64ea6e-ea04-4a7e-8307-38e29aae9432",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Building Tools with MCP: A Practical Guide"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-05 14:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "Ready to build your first MCP server? This practical guide walks through the key concepts: tools, resources, prompts, and the transport layer."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "listingImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "d5f29361-0fea-4d8d-860a-08b030750a20",
              "mediaKey": "0e38d454-0756-4942-b3cb-7ae1b02d7922",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "5a562a9b-5fb4-4077-9088-ffdc2ecac7f8",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "8178488e-435f-477c-ba50-538bdb393c2e",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "55b5e8d2-8130-4e65-aac3-890b23970225",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "794e06ff-d15b-4a87-9583-195965ac26bf",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "840baff3-d038-4aaf-b905-e24b1a9bc20c",
                "key": "5a562a9b-5fb4-4077-9088-ffdc2ecac7f8",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Building your first MCP server is remarkably straightforward. At its heart, an MCP server is just a program that speaks JSON-RPC over stdio or SSE. You define <strong>tools</strong> (functions the agent can call), <strong>resources</strong> (data the agent can read), and <strong>prompts</strong> (templates to scaffold interactions).</p><p>The official SDKs — available for Python, TypeScript, Java, and Kotlin — handle all the protocol plumbing. You focus on what your tool does, not how to marshal JSON.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "f6f04835-c513-4ccc-8f5d-cb024185c4da",
                "key": "8178488e-435f-477c-ba50-538bdb393c2e",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "e365fa5a-84e2-48ec-a130-7dc3058cdf41",
                        "mediaKey": "54dd747c-4abe-4b5c-8f87-b813a7b4a3f9",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "The MCP SDK makes server implementation a breeze"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "840baff3-d038-4aaf-b905-e24b1a9bc20c",
                "key": "55b5e8d2-8130-4e65-aac3-890b23970225",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Here is a quick example of what a tool definition looks like in the TypeScript SDK:</p><pre><code>server.tool(\"get-weather\",\n  { city: z.string() },\n  async ({ city }) =&gt; {\n    const data = await weatherApi.fetch(city)\n    return { content: [{ type: \"text\", text: data.summary }] }\n  }\n)</code></pre><p>That is it — one function call registers a tool. The SDK handles input validation (via Zod schemas), JSON-RPC wrapping, error reporting, and streaming. Your agent discovers it automatically on connection.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "d1735b0d-4d92-4f30-8bfc-e720238392ee",
                "key": "794e06ff-d15b-4a87-9583-195965ac26bf",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "quote",
                    "value": "Every MCP server you build is an investment in the AI ecosystem. One server, any client, infinite possibilities."
